var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"examples/doublegyre/#Double-gyre","page":"Double gyre","text":"The double gyre is a time-dependent model of a fluid flow, with two counter-rotating vortices in the rectangle 0 2 times 0 1, separated by a boundary that oscillates periodically in time. It is a classical example in the study of transport in fluids (Shadden et al., 2005). The velocity field is given by the stream function psi(x y t) = A sin(pi f(x t)) sin(pi y), with f(x t) = a(t) x^2 + b(t) x, a(t) = varepsilon sin(omega t) and b(t) = 1 - 2a(t):\n\nbegincases\n  dot x = -dfracpartial psipartial y = -pi A sin(pi f(x t)) cos(pi y) 1ex\n  dot y = dfracpartial psipartial x = pi A cos(pi f(x t)) sin(pi y) dfracpartial fpartial x(x t)\nendcases\n\nusing LagrangianDescriptors, OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nfunction doublegyre!(du, u, p, t)\n    A, ε, ω = p\n    x, y = u\n    a = ε * sin(ω * t)\n    b = 1 - 2a\n    f = a * x^2 + b * x\n    dfdx = 2a * x + b\n    du[1] = -π * A * sin(π * f) * cos(π * y)\n    du[2] = π * A * cos(π * f) * sin(π * y) * dfdx\nend\n\np = (0.1, 0.25, 2π / 10) # A, ε, ω\nprob = ODEProblem(doublegyre!, [0.0, 0.0], (0.0, 15.0), p)\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(0, 1, length = 201), x in range(0, 2, length = 401)]\n\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5())\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\"), figure = (size = (900, 450),))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nSince the flow is time dependent, the picture is that at the initial time t_0 = 0. The sharp curves are the stable and unstable manifolds of the hyperbolic trajectories near the oscillating boundary between the gyres. They intersect in a sequence of lobes, which carry fluid from one gyre to the other in each period.","title":"Double gyre"},{"category":"section","location":"postprocessing/#Alternative-via-post-processing","page":"Alternative via post-processing","text":"Alternatively, instead of augmenting the system and computing the descriptor along with the solutions, we can just solve the original system as usual and then integrate the infinitesimal descriptor over the solution. This can be done for a single solution, backward and/or forward in time, or for an ensemble of solutions of the original system.","title":"Alternative via post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"postprocessing/#Drawbacks","page":"Alternative via post-processing","text":"This is much simpler to implement, but it has some drawbacks:\n\nFirst, we will need to save each trajectory in full, for later post-processing, instead of only L_mathrmfwd(t_f) and L_mathrmbwd(t_f). Hence, this is much more memory demanding. Keep in mind we need to solve for a lot of trajectories.\nIf we want the backward Lagrangian descriptors, we also need to set up and solve the system backward.\nSome solutions may have some spread out time steps saved by the solver. We either have to force it to save on a fine time mesh for a faster and accurate integration, or we use the interpolation present in the solution, which is usually much slower to compute.","title":"Drawbacks"},{"category":"section","location":"postprocessing/#Example","page":"Alternative via post-processing","text":"Here we exemplify the idea by integrating a single solution of a scalar cubic equation using JuliaMath/QuadGK.jl.\n\nIt boils down to taking a solution sol of the ODEProblem and integrating the infinitesimal descriptor M with the appropriate arguments. In the case of a scalar ODE with an out-of-place right hand side f=f(u, p, t) and an infinitesimal descriptor M=M(u, p, t), this is simply\n\nM(t) = M(sol(t), sol.prob.p, t)\nfirst(quadgk(M, minimum(tspan), maximum(tspan)))\n\nFor a more generic implementation for ODESolution types, in either in-place or out-of-place cases, and for either backward or forward descriptors, one can define a function such as\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\nusing SciMLBase: isinplace\nusing QuadGK: quadgk\n\nfunction simple_lagrangian_descriptor(sol::ODESolution, M)\n    t0, tf = extrema(sol.prob.tspan)\n    integrand = isinplace(sol.prob) ?\n        function (t, du = similar(sol.prob.u0))\n            sol.prob.f(du, sol(t), sol.prob.p, t)\n            M(du, sol(t), sol.prob.p, t)\n        end :\n        function (t)\n            du = sol.prob.f(sol(t), sol.prob.p, t)\n            M(du, sol(t), sol.prob.p, t)\n        end\n    return first(quadgk(integrand, t0, tf))\nend\n\nA version of this, which also avoids some allocations, is available in the package as lagrangian_descriptor. We compare the two approaches, augmenting the system and post-processing its solutions, the latter with both versions, making sure they yield the same result, up to approximation errors:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, Test\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\nusing LagrangianDescriptors: augmentprob\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing QuadGK: quadgk\n\nf(u) = u - u^3\nf!(du, u, p, t) = (du .= f.(u))\n\nt0 = 0.0\ntf = 5.0\nn = 5\nu0 = 0.1 .+ 0.7rand(n)\n\ntspanfwd = (t0, tf)\nprobfwd = ODEProblem(f!, u0, tspanfwd)\n\ntspanbwd = (t0, 2t0 - tf)\nprobbwd = remake(probfwd, tspan = tspanbwd)\n\nsolfwd = solve(probfwd, Tsit5())\nsolbwd = solve(probbwd, Tsit5())\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\n\naugprob = augmentprob(probfwd, M)\naugsol = solve(augprob, Tsit5())\n\n@testset \"Augmented vs post-processing\" begin\n    @test augsol.u[end].lfwd ≈ lagrangian_descriptor(solfwd, M) rtol = 0.01\n    @test augsol.u[end].lbwd ≈ lagrangian_descriptor(solbwd, M) atol = 0.01\n    @test simple_lagrangian_descriptor(solfwd, M) ≈ lagrangian_descriptor(solfwd, M) rtol = 1e-6\n    @test simple_lagrangian_descriptor(solbwd, M) ≈ lagrangian_descriptor(solbwd, M) rtol = 1e-6\nend\nTest Summary:                | Pass  Total  Time\nAugmented vs post-processing |    4      4  1.8s","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"postprocessing/#Benchmark","page":"Alternative via post-processing","text":"And here is a benchmark with the above setup, with a single solution of the augmented system versus post-processing:\n\nusing BenchmarkTools\n\nbenchmarks = [\n    \"Create forward ODE problem\" => @benchmark(ODEProblem($f!, $u0, $tspanfwd)),\n    \"Remake for backward ODE problem\" => @benchmark(remake($probfwd, tspan = $tspanbwd)),\n    \"Solve forward ODE problem\" => @benchmark(solve($probfwd, $(Tsit5()))),\n    \"Solve backward ODE problem\" => @benchmark(solve($probbwd, $(Tsit5()))),\n    \"Create augmented ODE problem\" => @benchmark(augmentprob($probfwd, $M)),\n    \"Solve augmented ODE problem\" => @benchmark(solve($augprob, $(Tsit5()))),\n    \"Post-processing for forward Lagrangian descriptor\" => @benchmark(lagrangian_descriptor($solfwd, $M)),\n    \"Post-processing for backward Lagrangian descriptor\" => @benchmark(lagrangian_descriptor($solbwd, $M)),\n    \"Simple post-processing, forward\" => @benchmark(simple_lagrangian_descriptor($solfwd, $M)),\n    \"Simple post-processing, backward\" => @benchmark(simple_lagrangian_descriptor($solbwd, $M)),\n]\n\nText(join((rpad(name, 52) * lpad(BenchmarkTools.prettytime(minimum(b).time), 12) *\n    lpad(\"$(minimum(b).allocs) allocations\", 18) * lpad(BenchmarkTools.prettymemory(minimum(b).memory), 12)\n    for (name, b) in benchmarks), \"\\n\"))\nCreate forward ODE problem                            132.618 μs   576 allocations   44.55 KiB\nRemake for backward ODE problem                         5.998 ns     1 allocations    48 bytes\nSolve forward ODE problem                               4.543 μs   279 allocations   15.23 KiB\nSolve backward ODE problem                              4.601 μs   279 allocations   15.23 KiB\nCreate augmented ODE problem                            7.823 μs    79 allocations    3.41 KiB\nSolve augmented ODE problem                             8.729 μs   328 allocations   25.42 KiB\nPost-processing for forward Lagrangian descriptor      22.229 μs     8 allocations   992 bytes\nPost-processing for backward Lagrangian descriptor     13.112 μs     8 allocations   992 bytes\nSimple post-processing, forward                        46.170 μs  2614 allocations  123.12 KiB\nSimple post-processing, backward                       26.657 μs  1534 allocations   72.50 KiB\n\nWe see that solving the augmented system, with the forward and backward evolutions together, takes about as long as solving the forward and backward equations separately, while it also computes the Lagrangian descriptors.\n\nOn the other hand, solving the forward and backward equations separately requires a post-processing step for each of the forward and backward evolutions to obtain the Lagrangian descriptors, and each of these steps takes longer than solving the augmented system itself. Altogether, this route takes several times as long as solving the augmented system, and it needs the full solutions to be saved, while the augmented system only needs the values of the descriptors at the final time. And this was done for a single trajectory. Imagine it for an ensemble of solutions.\n\nThe package's lagrangian_descriptor is faster than the simple version above, and allocates much less, since it interpolates the solution in place and evaluates it only once at each time.","title":"Benchmark"},{"category":"section","location":"method/#method","page":"The method of Lagrangian descriptors","text":"The dynamics of evolutionary systems can be quite intricate. The method of Lagrangian Descriptors helps to visualize the complicated behavior of such systems and make sense of it. In a recent article, Wiggins and García-Garrido (2022) call it [a method for] painting the phase portrait (of a dynamical system).","title":"The method of Lagrangian descriptors"},{"category":"section","location":"method/#The-idea","page":"The method of Lagrangian descriptors","text":"The method is akin to dropping colored ink in a fluid flow, tracking the dye as it is transported by the flow, and revealing the pattern created after a certain period of time. The difference being that the color doesn't get diffused as in a real fluid, so the image doesn't get blurred and one gets a clearer picture.\n\nIt is similar to drawing a phase portrait, which displays a collection of orbits, but in this method each orbit is painted according to its dynamic behavior, better revealing the overall picture.\n\nThis \"coloring\" is obtained by integrating a local, or infinitesimal descriptor M=M(t u) along a solution u=u(t) of the system. One common choice for the infinitesimal descriptor is the velocity of the trajectory, so that, upon integration along the solution over a given period of time, the Lagrangian descriptor becomes the arc-length of that portion of the solution. Depending on whether the solution is at a fixed point, or belongs to a stable or unstable manifold, and so on, the arc-length would be closer for solutions with similar behavior, revealing the common patterns of the dynamics.\n\nThus, if u=u(t) is a solution over a time span (t_0 t_f), with t_f  t_0, then the forward Lagrangian descriptor is given by\n\nL_mathrmfwd = int_t_0^t_f M(t u(t)) mathrmdt\n\nIf t_f  t_0, so that u=u(t) is a \"backward\" solution from t_0, then we obtain the backward Lagrangian descriptor\n\nL_mathrmbwd = int_t_f^t_0 M(t u(t)) mathrmdt = - int_t_0^t_f M(t u(t)) mathrmdt\n\nSince the system might be non-autonomous, these Lagrangian descriptors are referred to as descriptors near t_0.","title":"The idea"},{"category":"section","location":"method/#Computing-the-descriptors","page":"The method of Lagrangian descriptors","text":"Notice the computation of the Lagrangian descriptors only depend on a given solution and on the infinitesimal descriptor, so that they can be computed a posteriori. However, this is not the most efficient way of computing them. For a more efficient implementation, one writes the integrals for the Lagrangian descriptors as differential equations, i.e.\n\nfracmathrmdL_mathrmfwdmathrmdt = M(t u(t))\n\nand similarly for the backward Lagrangian descriptor. Then, one solves the (partially) coupled system to obtain the integrated descriptor.\n\nMore explicitly, when both forward and backward Lagrangians are desired, we may write a four-component system for solving both the backward and forward solutions and the backward and forward Lagrangians at the same time:\n\nbegincases\n  displaystyle fracmathrmdumathrmdt = f(u t)  \n  displaystyle fracmathrmdvmathrmdt = -f(v 2t_0 - t)  \n  displaystyle fracmathrmdL_mathrmfwdmathrmdt = M(u t)  \n  displaystyle fracmathrmdL_mathrmbwdmathrmdt = M(v 2t_0 - t)\nendcases\n\nwith the set of initial conditions\n\nu(t_0) = v(t_0) = u_0 quad L_mathrmfwd(t_0) = L_mathrmbwd(t_0) = 0\n\nFor a given time interval (t_0 t_f), with t_f  t_0, notice that u=u(t) solves the system forward, over the interval (t_0 t_f), while v=v(t) is defined over (t_0 t_f) but corresponds to solving the system backward, starting at t_0 and ending at 2t_0 - t_f = t_0 - (t_f - t_0), both with the same initial condition u_0, i.e. the augmented system computes the forward and backward parts of the same trajectory, over the same length of time, but in different directions.","title":"Computing the descriptors"},{"category":"section","location":"method/#Implementation","page":"The method of Lagrangian descriptors","text":"The API is simple, it amounts to i) building a LagrangianDescriptorProblem, ii) solving it with an overload of solve; and iii) plotting the solution with a plot recipe:\n\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, alg)\nplot(lagsol)\n\nwhere prob is a given problem, such as an ODEProblem; M is the infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor; uu0 is a collection of initial conditions; and alg is the algorithm chosen to solve prob, such as Tsit5().\n\nMore precisely, the implementation works as follows:\n\nOne builds a differential equation problem prob of a type defined in SciMLBase.jl, from SciML/DifferentialEquations.jl, say an ODEProblem for mathrmdumathrmdt = f(u p t) (in-place or out-of-place). \nOne chooses an infinitesimal descriptor of the form M=M(du, u, p, t) (or other form suitable to the given problem type), with scalar values, that will be integrated along a solution u(t) = u(t u_0), to yield the forward Lagrangian descriptor L_mathrmfwd(u_0) and the backward Lagrangian descriptor L_mathrmbwd(u_0), for a given initial condition u_0.\nOne chooses a collection uu0 of initial conditions (e.g. a Vector, or a more general Array or any iterator, with the elements of the collection being suitable initial conditions for the given problem), representing a mesh in phase space or a portion of a sub-manifold of the phase space to be \"painted\" by the method.\nThen we pass them to lagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), to create the associated Lagrangian descriptor problem lagprob.\nLagrangianDescriptorProblem uses prob.f, prob.p, prob.u0, prob.tspan, and any prob.kwargs to create, via ComponentArrays, a new problem (e.g. a new ODEProblem) for the augmented system.\nThe augmented problem has, by default, four components, for solving the original equation forward and backward in time, and for solving the Lagrangian descriptors forward and backward in time. The way the augmented systems is constructed is via ComponentArrays.jl, so the augmented system is a ComponentVector, with components fwd, bwd, lfwd, and lbwd, respectively.\nOne may choose the keyword direction = :forward or direction = :backward to build the augmented system in only one direction, with the default being direction = :both.\nLagrangianDescriptorProblem then wraps an EnsembleProblem for the augmented system and with the given collection uu_0 of initial conditions to be used at each trajectory of the ensemble;\nSolve the LagrangianDescriptorProblem by invoking lagsol = solve(lagprob, alg, args..., kwargs...), with the appropriate algorithm and any other argument for the ensemble solve of the underlying ensemble problem lagprob.ensprob. \nsolve returns a LagrangianDescriptorSolution containing the associated collection of (forward and backward) Lagrangian descriptor values at the final time of each simulation (which is t_f for the forward components and corresponds to 2t_0 - t_f for the backward ones). This solution has the following fields:\nenssol: the EnsembleSolution of the associated ensemble problem, where each element of enssol.u is a NamedTuple with the Lagrangian descriptors lfwd and lbwd (or only one of them, depending on the direction) at the final time of the simulation (i.e. t_f for the forward Lagrangian and 2t_0 - t_f for the backward), and the solver's return code retcode;\nuu0: the given collection of initial conditions;\ndirection: the direction chosen, which defaults to :both, but can be also :forward or :backward;\nterminated, terminated_forward and terminated_backward: which trajectories, and in which directions, were stopped by the keyword max_trajectory_value of solve;\nretcodes: the solver's return code of each trajectory, to detect trajectories that did not finish normally (e.g. with .!SciMLBase.successful_retcode.(lagsol.retcodes)).\nFinally, one can use plot to visualize either the forward, or the backward, or the sum (total), or even the difference, of the forward and backward Lagrangian descriptor, via a plot recipe for the LagrangianDescriptorSolution, i.e. by calling plot(lagsol) or plot(lagsol, direction), where direction is either :forward, :backward, :total or :difference, with either Plots.jl or Makie.jl.\n\nFor more on the method, see the References, and, for the choices that shape the pictures, the page on choosing the descriptor and the time window.","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"The typical use of the package consists of three steps:\n\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)  # 1. set up the problem\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, alg)                        # 2. solve it\nplot(lagsol)                                        # 3. visualize the descriptors\n\nwhere prob is a differential equation problem, M is an infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor, uu0 is a collection of initial conditions, and alg is an algorithm to solve prob. The Lagrangian descriptors at each initial condition are then given by lagsol(), or lagsol(:forward), lagsol(:backward), and so on.","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Problems","page":"API","text":"","title":"Problems"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Lagrangian-descriptors","page":"API","text":"The Lagrangian descriptors are given either by the integral of an infinitesimal descriptor M along the solutions, or by the sum of the increments of the solutions over a time grid, with an IncrementDescriptor.\n\nThe infinitesimal descriptor M is any function with the arguments below, where du is the value of the right-hand side, or drift, of the equation at the state u, with parameters p, at time t:\n\nProblem Infinitesimal descriptor du\nODEProblem M(du, u, p, t) f(u, p, t)\nRODEProblem M(du, u, p, t, W) f(u, p, t, W), with the noise W\nSDEProblem M(du, u, p, t) the drift f(u, p, t)\n\nA common choice is the norm of the velocity, M(du, u, p, t) = norm(du), for which the Lagrangian descriptor is the length of the solution over the time interval.","title":"Lagrangian descriptors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Solving","page":"API","text":"","title":"Solving"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Solutions","page":"API","text":"","title":"Solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Plotting","page":"API","text":"A LagrangianDescriptorSolution can be plotted with Makie or Plots, e.g. with plot(lagsol) or plot(lagsol, direction); see Plotting.","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Lower-level-functions","page":"API","text":"The augmented problems solved for each initial condition, and the computation of the Lagrangian descriptors of a single solution of the original problem, by post-processing (see Alternative via post-processing).","title":"Lower-level functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LagrangianDescriptors.LagrangianDescriptorProblem","page":"API","text":"LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0; direction = :both)\n\nA Lagrangian descriptor problem: the computation of the Lagrangian descriptors of the differential equation problem prob, given by the infinitesimal descriptor M, at each initial condition in the collection uu0. Solve it with solve to obtain a LagrangianDescriptorSolution.\n\nArguments\n\nprob: an ODEProblem, a RODEProblem or an SDEProblem. Its time span (t0, tf) sets the time windows: [t0, tf] forward and [2t0 - tf, t0] backward. Its initial condition is replaced by the elements of uu0, and everything else, such as its parameters and keyword arguments, is kept.\nM: the infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor, integrated along the forward and backward solutions. Its signature depends on the problem:\nODEProblem: M(du, u, p, t), where du is the value of the right-hand side;\nRODEProblem: M(du, u, p, t, W), where W is the value of the noise;\nSDEProblem: M(du, u, p, t), where du is the value of the drift.\nAlternatively, an IncrementDescriptor, for the descriptor given by the increments of the solution.\nuu0: a collection of initial conditions, such as a vector, or a matrix for a grid of initial conditions of a two-dimensional problem. Each element is used as an initial condition of prob, so it is a number for a scalar problem, or a vector otherwise.\ndirection: :both (default), :forward or :backward; the directions of time in which the Lagrangian descriptors are computed.\n\nFields\n\nensprob: the EnsembleProblem solved by solve with its default keyword arguments. Its trajectories solve the augmented problem built by augmentprob, and their outputs are the Lagrangian descriptors at the end of the time interval, along with the solver's return codes.\nuu0: the given collection of initial conditions.\ndirection: the given direction.\nprob and M: the given problem and descriptor, from which solve builds the ensemble problem for other keyword arguments.\n\nExample\n\nLagrangian descriptors of a periodically forced Duffing equation, over a grid of initial conditions:\n\nusing LagrangianDescriptors, OrdinaryDiffEq, Plots\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nfunction f!(du, u, p, t)\n    x, y = u\n    A, ω = p\n    du[1] = y\n    du[2] = x - x^3 + A * cos(ω * t)\nend\n\nu0 = [0.0, 0.0]\ntspan = (0.0, 13.0)\nA = 0.3; ω = π; p = (A, ω)\n\nprob = ODEProblem(f!, u0, tspan, p)\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.0, 1.0, length = 301), x in range(-1.8, 1.8, length = 301)]\n\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5())\nplot(lagsol)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianDescriptors.LagrangianDescriptorProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LagrangianDescriptors.IncrementDescriptor","page":"API","text":"IncrementDescriptor(; Δt, p = 0.5)\n\nLagrangian descriptor given by the sum of the p-th powers of the increments of the solution over a time grid with step Δt, as defined for stochastic differential equations in Balibrea-Iniesta, Lopesino, Wiggins and Mancho, Lagrangian Descriptors for Stochastic Differential Equations: A Tool for Revealing the Phase Portrait of Stochastic Dynamical Systems (2016).\n\nIt is passed in place of the infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor M when building a LagrangianDescriptorProblem:\n\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, IncrementDescriptor(Δt = 0.05, p = 0.5), uu0)\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, EM(), dt = 0.05)\n\nWith tspan = (t0, tf) and the grid t_j = t0 + j Δt, j = 0, …, N, with t_N = tf, the forward Lagrangian descriptor is\n\nL_mathrmfwd = sum_j=0^N-1 sum_k left u^k(t_j+1) - u^k(t_j) right^p\n\nwhere k runs over the components of the solution, and the backward one is the same sum along the backward solution, over the grid t0 - j Δt, j = 0, …, N. Their sum is the descriptor of the paper, over the time window [2t0 - tf, tf].\n\nThe time span tf - t0 must be a multiple of Δt. The solution is computed on the grid points exactly (they are passed to the solver as saveat and tstops), not interpolated, since interpolation would smooth out the noise. The paper uses the Euler–Maruyama method with step Δt, but any algorithm can be used.\n\nFor a stochastic equation, the descriptor depends on Δt and does not converge as Δt → 0, since the paths of a Wiener process have infinite length. The paper uses 0 < p ≤ 1. For p = 1 and a differential equation, it converges to the length of the solution as Δt → 0.\n\nIt also works for ODEProblems and RODEProblems. The mean over many realizations of the noise, also used in the paper, is obtained with the keyword realizations of solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianDescriptors.IncrementDescriptor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CommonSolve.solve","page":"API","text":"solve(prob::LagrangianDescriptorProblem, alg, args...; max_trajectory_value = Inf, realizations = 1, shared_noise = false, kwargs...)\n\nSolve a LagrangianDescriptorProblem, returning a LagrangianDescriptorSolution.\n\nEach initial condition in prob.uu0 is solved with the algorithm alg, as one trajectory of the ensemble problem prob.ensprob. The remaining positional arguments args and keyword arguments kwargs are passed on to the ensemble solve, and so to the solve of each trajectory. For instance, an ensemble algorithm, such as EnsembleThreads() (the default), EnsembleSerial() or EnsembleDistributed(), can be given after alg, and tolerances (abstol, reltol), a time step (dt), or a callback are given as keyword arguments. The number of trajectories is set from prob.uu0 and realizations.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmax_trajectory_value::Real = Inf: if finite, the forward and the backward solutions are each stopped as soon as the absolute value of any of their components exceeds it. This is useful for systems whose solutions diverge, e.g. dissipative systems backward in time. The two directions are treated separately: a direction that is stopped keeps the Lagrangian descriptor accumulated up to then, while the other direction goes on. The stopped directions are marked in the fields terminated_forward and terminated_backward of the solution, and terminated marks the trajectories with any stopped direction.\nrealizations::Integer = 1: the number of times each initial condition is solved. For random or stochastic equations, each realization has different noise, and the solution gives the mean of the Lagrangian descriptors over the realizations, as in Balibrea-Iniesta, Lopesino, Wiggins and Mancho (2016). The return codes and the terminated flags of the solution are kept for each of the realizations * length(prob.uu0) solves.\nshared_noise::Bool = false: for an SDEProblem or a RODEProblem, whether all initial conditions of a realization share the same noise path, as in the definition of the Lagrangian descriptors of stochastic equations of Balibrea-Iniesta, Lopesino, Wiggins and Mancho (2016), for a fixed realization of the noise. By default, each initial condition has independent noise. If the problem has a seed, the noise is reproducible in both cases.\n\nExamples\n\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5())                          # an ODE problem\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5(), EnsembleSerial(); abstol = 1e-8, reltol = 1e-8)\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5(); max_trajectory_value = 1e3)\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, RandomHeun(), dt = 1 / 100)       # a RODE problem\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, SOSRI(); realizations = 50)       # an SDE problem\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, EM(), dt = 0.01; shared_noise = true, realizations = 10)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LagrangianDescriptors.LagrangianDescriptorSolution","page":"API","text":"LagrangianDescriptorSolution\n\nThe solution of a LagrangianDescriptorProblem, returned by solve.\n\nLagrangian descriptors\n\nThe Lagrangian descriptors are obtained by calling the solution, lagsol(direction); see below. They are plotted with plot(lagsol); see the section on plotting.\n\nFields\n\nenssol: the EnsembleSolution of the ensemble problem prob.ensprob. Each element of enssol.u is a NamedTuple with the Lagrangian descriptors lfwd and/or lbwd at the end of a trajectory, depending on the direction, and the solver's return code retcode.\nuu0: the collection of initial conditions of the problem.\ndirection: the direction of the problem, :both, :forward or :backward.\nterminated: a BitVector marking the trajectories with a direction stopped by the keyword max_trajectory_value of solve.\nretcodes: the solver's return code (a SciMLBase.ReturnCode.T) of each trajectory. Trajectories that did not finish normally, e.g. because they reached maxiters or became unstable, are found with .!SciMLBase.successful_retcode.(lagsol.retcodes); their Lagrangian descriptors are not reliable.\nterminated_forward and terminated_backward: BitVectors marking the trajectories whose forward or backward solution, respectively, was stopped by max_trajectory_value; their descriptors in that direction only measure the solution up to then.\n\nWhen solved with several realizations (see solve), enssol.u and the flags and return codes have one entry per solve: those of all initial conditions of the first realization, then of the second one, and so on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianDescriptors.LagrangianDescriptorSolution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LagrangianDescriptors.augmentprob","page":"API","text":"augmentprob(prob::ODEProblem, M; direction::Symbol = :both, max_trajectory_value::Real = Inf)\n\nCreate an augmented ODEProblem by aggregating the forward and backward Lagrangian descriptors to the given ODEProblem.\n\nMore precisely, with given f = prob.f, u0 = prob.u0, and tspan = prob.tspan, an augmented ODEProblem is created that solves the forward and backward ODE and the forward and backward Lagrangian descriptors simultaneously.\n\nWith tspan = (t0, tf), the forward problem is solved over [t0, tf] and the backward problem over [2t0 - tf, t0], both starting from u0 at time t0. The backward problem is integrated via the change of variables t ↦ 2t0 - t, so that both run along the same time interval tspan.\n\nThe (global) Lagrangian descriptors are based on the provided local descriptor M=M(du, u, p, t). The (forward/backward) Lagrangian descriptor is the time-integration of the local descriptor along the (forward/backward) trajectory.\n\nIf direction=:forward or direction=:backward are given, then only the forward or backward problem is constructed in the augmented system. The default is direction=:both, where both directions are considered.\n\nIf max_trajectory_value is finite, each direction is frozen once the absolute value of a component of its solution exceeds it: its solution and its Lagrangian descriptor stop changing, while the other direction goes on.\n\n\n\n\n\naugmentprob(prob::RODEProblem, M; direction::Symbol = :both, max_trajectory_value::Real = Inf)\n\nCreate an augmented RODEProblem by aggregating the forward and backward Lagrangian descriptors to the given RODEProblem.\n\nMore precisely, with given f = prob.f, u0 = prob.u0, W = prob.noise, and tspan = prob.tspan, an augmented RODEProblem is created that solves the forward and backward RODE and the forward and backward Lagrangian descriptors simultaneously.\n\nWith tspan = (t0, tf), the forward problem is solved over [t0, tf] and the backward problem over [2t0 - tf, t0], both starting from u0 at time t0. The backward problem is integrated via the change of variables t ↦ 2t0 - t, so that both run along the same time interval tspan.\n\nUnless prob has a given noise process, the noise is the solver's default Wiener process starting at t0, with the shape of rand_prototype, or of u0 if rand_prototype is not given, as when solving prob directly. The forward and backward problems are then driven by independent Wiener processes, as for a two-sided Wiener process, whose paths over [2t0 - tf, t0] and over [t0, tf] are independent. In the augmented problem, the noise is a vector with a copy for each direction, and f and M receive the noise of their direction, with the shape of rand_prototype or u0 (a scalar for a scalar u0).\n\nIf prob has a given noise process, an independent copy of it is not available in general, so both directions are driven by that same process, evaluated at the forward time t.\n\nThe (global) Lagrangian descriptors are based on the provided local descriptor M=M(du, u, p, t, W). The (forward/backward) Lagrangian descriptor is the time-integration of the local descriptor along the (forward/backward) trajectory.\n\nIf direction=:forward or direction=:backward are given, then only the forward or backward problem is constructed in the augmented system. The default is direction=:both, where both directions are considered.\n\nIf max_trajectory_value is finite, each direction is frozen once the absolute value of a component of its solution exceeds it: its solution and its Lagrangian descriptor stop changing, while the other direction goes on.\n\n\n\n\n\naugmentprob(prob::SDEProblem, M; direction::Symbol = :both, max_trajectory_value::Real = Inf)\n\nCreate an augmented SDEProblem by aggregating the forward and backward Lagrangian descriptors to the given SDEProblem.\n\nMore precisely, with given drift f = prob.f, diffusion g = prob.g, u0 = prob.u0, and tspan = prob.tspan, an augmented SDEProblem is created that solves the forward and backward SDE and the forward and backward Lagrangian descriptors simultaneously.\n\nWith tspan = (t0, tf), the forward problem is solved over [t0, tf] and the backward problem over [2t0 - tf, t0], both starting from u0 at time t0. The backward problem is integrated via the change of variables t ↦ 2t0 - t, so that both run along the same time interval tspan: its drift is -f and its diffusion is g, both evaluated at the time 2t0 - t.\n\nThe noise driving the backward problem is independent of the noise driving the forward problem, as for a two-sided Wiener process, whose increments over [2t0 - tf, t0] and over [t0, tf] are independent. With diagonal noise, each component of the augmented system has its own Wiener process. With non-diagonal noise, given by an n × m noise_rate_prototype, the augmented noise matrix has the diffusion of the forward problem and that of the backward problem in separate columns, so each has its own m noise channels.\n\nThe (global) Lagrangian descriptors are based on the provided local descriptor M=M(du, u, p, t), where du is the drift f(u, p, t). The (forward/backward) Lagrangian descriptor is the time-integration of the local descriptor along the (forward/backward) stochastic trajectory.\n\nIf direction=:forward or direction=:backward are given, then only the forward or backward problem is constructed in the augmented system. The default is direction=:both, where both directions are considered.\n\nIf max_trajectory_value is finite, each direction is frozen once the absolute value of a component of its solution exceeds it: its solution and its Lagrangian descriptor stop changing, while the other direction goes on.\n\nCustom noise processes (the noise keyword of SDEProblem) are not supported, since the augmented system needs noise with a different dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianDescriptors.augmentprob"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LagrangianDescriptors.lagrangian_descriptor","page":"API","text":"lagrangian_descriptor(sol::ODESolution, M)\n\nComputes the Lagrangian descriptor of a single trajectory of an ODEProblem.\n\nIt takes the provided infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor of the form M=M(du, u, p, t), along with the time interval given in sol.prob.tspan, to compute\n\nL = int_t_0^t_f M(du(t) u(t) p t) mathrmdt\n\nwhere t_0 and t_f are the initial and final times in sol.prob.tspan, u(t) = sol(t), p = sol.prob.p, and du(t) is computed from sol in a way dependent on whether sol.prob is in place or out of place.\n\nThe time integration is done via QuadGK.quadgk.\n\n\n\n\n\nlagrangian_descriptor(sol::RODESolution, M)\n\nComputes the Lagrangian descriptor of a single trajectory of a RODEProblem or of an SDEProblem (whose solutions are also RODESolutions).\n\nIt takes the provided infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor in the form M=M(du, u, p, t, W), along with the time interval given in sol.prob.tspan, to compute\n\nL = int_t_0^t_f M(du(t) u(t) p t W(t)) mathrmdt\n\nwhere t_0 and t_f are the initial and final times in sol.prob.tspan, u(t) = sol(t), p = sol.prob.p, W(t) = first(sol.W(t)), and du(t) is either du = sol.prob.f(sol(t), sol.prob.p, t, first(sol.W(t))) or given by sol.prob.f(du, sol(t), sol.prob.p, t, first(sol.W(t))), depending on whether sol.prob is in place or out of place.\n\nFor an SDEProblem, the infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor has the form M=M(du, u, p, t), where du is the drift sol.prob.f along the solution, without the noise argument, as in augmentprob for SDEProblems.\n\nQuadGK.quadgk is an adaptive integration method and does not perform well on solutions of random or stochastic equations, so the integration, in these case, is performed via a simple trapezoidal rule on the mesh given by sol.t, which may be far com accurate depending on the mesh.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianDescriptors.lagrangian_descriptor"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#Choosing-the-descriptor-and-the-time-window","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"The picture painted by the Lagrangian descriptors depends on a few choices: the infinitesimal descriptor M, the length of the time window, the directions of time, and the grid of initial conditions. This page shows their effect on a simple example, and gives some guidance on how to choose them.","title":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#A-model-problem","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"We use the Duffing equation ddot x = x - x^3, written as the system\n\nbegincases\n  dot x = y \n  dot y = x - x^3\nendcases\n\nIt has a saddle point at the origin, whose stable and unstable manifolds form two homoclinic loops, and two centers, at (pm 1 0), surrounded by periodic orbits.\n\nusing LagrangianDescriptors, OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nfunction duffing!(du, u, p, t)\n    du[1] = u[2]\n    du[2] = u[1] - u[1]^3\nend\n\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.0, 1.0, length = 201), x in range(-1.8, 1.8, length = 201)]\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\n\nprob = ODEProblem(duffing!, [0.0, 0.0], (0.0, 5.0))\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5())","title":"A model problem"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#Why-the-method-works","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"With M(du, u, p, t) = norm(du), the forward Lagrangian descriptor is the length of the solution over the time window. Consider two nearby initial conditions on opposite sides of the stable manifold of the saddle. Both approach the saddle, where they slow down, but then they leave it in opposite directions, along the two branches of the unstable manifold, so their lengths differ. Initial conditions on the stable manifold itself stay close to the saddle, where they move slowly. As a result, the forward descriptor, as a function of the initial condition, is not smooth across the stable manifold, which shows up as a sharp line in the picture. In the same way, the backward descriptor reveals the unstable manifolds. See Mancho et al. (2013) and Lopesino et al. (2017) for the theory.","title":"Why the method works"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#Forward,-backward-and-total","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"The forward descriptors reveal the stable manifolds, the backward ones the unstable manifolds, and their sum, the default, shows both:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1200, 400))\nfor (i, direction) in enumerate((:forward, :backward, :total))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i]; title = string(direction), xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\n    heatmap!(ax, lagsol, direction)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nIf only the stable or only the unstable manifolds are of interest, it is cheaper to solve in a single direction, with the keyword direction = :forward or direction = :backward of LagrangianDescriptorProblem.","title":"Forward, backward and total"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#The-infinitesimal-descriptor","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"The most common choice is the norm of the velocity, norm(du), which makes the descriptor the length of the solution. Another common choice is the \"p-norm\" sum_i dot u_i^p, with 0  p leq 1, introduced by Lopesino et al. (2017), for which the singular features at the manifolds are sharper and can be characterized rigorously. The value p = 12 is often used:\n\nMhalf(du, u, p, t) = sum(x -> sqrt(abs(x)), du)\nlagsol_half = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, Mhalf, uu0), Tsit5())\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"norm(du)\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, lagsol)\nax = Axis(fig[1, 2]; title = \"p-norm with p = 1/2\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, lagsol_half)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nAny function with the arguments of the table in the API can be used, e.g. depending on the state u instead of the velocity, or on a single component of the velocity, to highlight particular features of a problem.","title":"The infinitesimal descriptor"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#The-time-window","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"The time span (t0, tf) of the problem sets the time windows: t_0 t_f forward and 2t_0 - t_f t_0 backward, both of length tau = t_f - t_0. A short window only reveals the structures near each initial condition, and a long one reveals more of them, as the solutions explore more of the phase space:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1200, 400))\nfor (i, τ) in enumerate((1.0, 3.0, 8.0))\n    sol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(remake(prob, tspan = (0.0, τ)), M, uu0), Tsit5())\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i]; title = \"τ = $τ\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\n    heatmap!(ax, sol)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nA good window is comparable to the time scales of the dynamics of interest, e.g. a few periods of the periodic orbits, or a few times the time it takes to approach a saddle. With longer windows, the features become thinner and more intricate, which needs a finer grid of initial conditions, and solutions of unbounded systems may grow too large (see below). It is often worth trying a few values of tau.\n\nFor a non-autonomous system, the descriptors are those at the initial time t_0: the structures move with t_0, and changing the start of the time span changes the picture, not only the window.","title":"The time window"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#Diverging-solutions","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"For some systems, such as dissipative systems backward in time, or systems in which solutions can escape to infinity, some solutions grow very large within the time window. Their descriptors then dominate the color scale, and the solver may take many small steps or fail. The keyword max_trajectory_value of solve stops the forward and the backward solutions, separately, once one of their components exceeds the given value, keeping the descriptor accumulated up to then. With damping, for instance, the solutions decay forward in time, but some of them grow backward in time:\n\nfunction damped_duffing!(du, u, p, t)\n    du[1] = u[2]\n    du[2] = u[1] - u[1]^3 - 0.3u[2]\nend\n\nprob_damped = ODEProblem(damped_duffing!, [0.0, 0.0], (0.0, 8.0))\nlagsol_damped = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob_damped, M, uu0), Tsit5(); max_trajectory_value = 10.0)\nLagrangianDescriptorSolution\n  initial conditions: 40401 (201 × 201) of type Vector{Float64}\n  direction: :both\n  stopped by `max_trajectory_value`: 0 forward, 68 backward\n\nThe numbers of forward and backward solutions stopped early are\n\ncount(lagsol_damped.terminated_forward), count(lagsol_damped.terminated_backward)\n(0, 68)\n\nThe descriptors of the stopped solutions only measure how fast they diverge, so it may be clearer to leave them out of the picture, setting them to NaN:\n\nxs = range(-1.8, 1.8, length = 201)\nys = range(-1.0, 1.0, length = 201)\nLbwd = lagsol_damped(:backward)\nLbwd[lagsol_damped.terminated_backward] .= NaN\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"forward\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, lagsol_damped, :forward)\nax = Axis(fig[1, 2]; title = \"backward, bounded solutions\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\n# Makie's `heatmap(xs, ys, z)` has `z[i, j]` at `(xs[i], ys[j])`, hence the `permutedims`\nheatmap!(ax, xs, ys, permutedims(reshape(Lbwd, size(uu0))))\nfig\nFigure()\n\nMore generally, the return codes in lagsol.retcodes tell which solutions did not finish normally, e.g. because they reached the maximum number of steps; their descriptors are not reliable. See García-Garrido and García-Luengo (2022) for more on dissipative systems.","title":"Diverging solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#The-grid-of-initial-conditions","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"Features thinner than the spacing of the grid are missed, and the cost of the computation grows with the number of initial conditions. A practical approach is to start with a coarse grid over a large region, and then to refine it, or to zoom into the regions of interest:\n\ncoarse = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.0, 1.0, length = 41), x in range(-1.8, 1.8, length = 41)]\nzoom = [[x, y] for y in range(-0.3, 0.3, length = 201), x in range(-0.3, 0.3, length = 201)]\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"41 × 41 grid\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, coarse), Tsit5()))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 2]; title = \"zoom near the saddle\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, zoom), Tsit5()))\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe initial conditions do not need to form a grid: any collection can be used, e.g. points along a curve, or a two-dimensional slice of initial conditions of a higher-dimensional system, chosen for plotting with the keyword idxs (see the section on plotting in the API).","title":"The grid of initial conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#Random-and-stochastic-equations","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"For random and stochastic equations, the picture also depends on the realization of the noise. By default, each initial condition has independent noise. The keyword shared_noise = true of solve gives the same noise path to all initial conditions, for the picture of a single realization of the noise, as in Balibrea-Iniesta et al. (2016). Averaging over realizations, with the keyword realizations, shows the structures shared by the realizations. The IncrementDescriptor gives the descriptor of Balibrea-Iniesta et al. (2016), given by the increments of the solutions over a time grid with step Δt, whose values depend on Δt, so it should be kept fixed when comparing pictures. See the stochastic Duffing equation for an example.","title":"Random and stochastic equations"},{"category":"section","location":"choosing/#Summary","page":"Choosing the descriptor and the time window","text":"Start with M(du, u, p, t) = norm(du), or the p-norm with p = 12 for sharper features.\nChoose the time window from the time scales of the dynamics, and try a few values.\nUse the forward descriptors for stable manifolds, the backward ones for unstable manifolds, and both for the full picture.\nStop diverging solutions with max_trajectory_value, and check lagsol.retcodes.\nStart with a coarse grid, then refine it or zoom in.","title":"Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/doublependulum/#Double-pendulum","page":"Double pendulum","text":"The double pendulum, with two equal masses on rods of equal length, in units where the masses, the lengths and the acceleration of gravity are equal to one, is described by the angles theta_1 and theta_2 of the rods with the vertical and their angular velocities omega_1 and omega_2:\n\nbegincases\n  dottheta_1 = omega_1 \n  dottheta_2 = omega_2 \n  dotomega_1 = dfrac-3sintheta_1 - sin(theta_1 - 2theta_2) - 2sinDelta(omega_2^2 + omega_1^2cosDelta)2 - cos 2Delta 1ex\n  dotomega_2 = dfrac2sinDelta(2omega_1^2 + 2costheta_1 + omega_2^2cosDelta)2 - cos 2Delta\nendcases\n\nwith Delta = theta_1 - theta_2. Its phase space is four-dimensional, and we compute the Lagrangian descriptors on the slice of initial conditions at rest, omega_1 = omega_2 = 0, in the components theta_1 and theta_2.\n\nusing LagrangianDescriptors, OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nfunction doublependulum!(du, u, p, t)\n    θ1, θ2, ω1, ω2 = u\n    Δ = θ1 - θ2\n    den = 2 - cos(2Δ)\n    du[1] = ω1\n    du[2] = ω2\n    du[3] = (-3sin(θ1) - sin(θ1 - 2θ2) - 2sin(Δ) * (ω2^2 + ω1^2 * cos(Δ))) / den\n    du[4] = 2sin(Δ) * (2ω1^2 + 2cos(θ1) + ω2^2 * cos(Δ)) / den\nend\n\nprob = ODEProblem(doublependulum!, zeros(4), (0.0, 10.0))\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = [[θ1, θ2, 0.0, 0.0] for θ2 in range(-π, π, length = 301), θ1 in range(-π, π, length = 301)]\n\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5())\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (xlabel = L\"\\theta_1\", ylabel = L\"\\theta_2\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nNear the stable equilibrium at the origin, the motion is regular, made of small oscillations, and the descriptor varies smoothly. Released from larger angles, the double pendulum has enough energy for its rods to flip over, and the motion becomes chaotic, with intricate structures that become finer as the time window grows.","title":"Double pendulum"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Overview","text":"A gallery of examples of the method of Lagrangian descriptors, from simple systems with known phase portraits to chaotic and stochastic ones. Each example sets up the problem, computes the Lagrangian descriptors, and discusses the picture.\n\nExample Features\nSaddle point linear system; stable and unstable manifolds\nPendulum conservative system; separatrices between oscillations and rotations\nVan der Pol oscillator limit cycle; solutions diverging backward in time\nDouble gyre time-dependent fluid flow; lobes and transport\nLorenz system three-dimensional chaotic system; two-dimensional slices of initial conditions\nDouble pendulum four-dimensional chaotic Hamiltonian system\nStochastic Duffing equation stochastic differential equation, with multiplicative noise; a single realization and the mean over realizations\n\nThe periodically forced Duffing equation, a random Duffing equation and a Duffing equation with additive noise are treated step by step in the tutorials on ODEs, RODEs and SDEs. For the choice of the infinitesimal descriptor, the time window and the grid of initial conditions, see Choosing the descriptor and the time window.","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial_SDEs/#Stochastic-Differential-Equations","page":"SDEs","text":"We consider, now, an example of applying the Lagrangian descriptor method to equations of the type SDEProblem.","title":"Stochastic Differential Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial_SDEs/#Duffing-equation-with-additive-noise","page":"SDEs","text":"We consider the Duffing equation with an additive noise in the velocity,\n\nbegincases\n  mathrmdx = y mathrmdt \n  mathrmdy = (x - x^3) mathrmdt + sigma mathrmdW_t\nendcases\n\nwhere W_t_t is a standard Wiener process and sigma is the intensity of the noise. Without the noise, the origin is a saddle point, whose stable and unstable manifolds form two homoclinic loops around the centers (pm 1 0).\n\nWith LagrangianDescriptors.jl, we i) start by setting up the equation as an SDEProblem; next, we ii) wrap that as a LagrangianDescriptorProblem; then we iii) solve it; and finally, we iv) plot the result with the built-in plot recipe.\n\nSo we first load the relevant packages:\n\nusing StochasticDiffEq\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\n\nNext we set up the SDEProblem, with the drift f! and the diffusion g!, here a diagonal noise, which is zero in the first component, and a seed for reproducible results:\n\nfunction f!(du, u, p, t)\n    x, y = u\n    du[1] = y\n    du[2] = x - x^3\nend\n\nfunction g!(du, u, σ, t)\n    du[1] = 0.0\n    du[2] = σ\nend\n\nσ = 0.05\nu0 = [0.0, 0.0]\ntspan = (0.0, 5.0)\n\nprob = SDEProblem(f!, g!, u0, tspan, σ; seed = 123)\n\u001b[38;2;86;182;194mSDEProblem\u001b[0m with uType \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mVector{Float64}\u001b[0m and tType \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mFloat64\u001b[0m. In-place: \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mtrue\u001b[0m\nNon-trivial mass matrix: \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mfalse\u001b[0m\ntimespan: (0.0, 5.0)\nu0: 2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.0\n 0.0\n\nWith the SDE problem setup, we choose an infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor, a collection of initial conditions on the phase space, which is the region to be \"painted\", and finally we build the LagrangianDescriptorProblem. For an SDEProblem, the infinitesimal descriptor has the signature M(du, u, p, t), where du is the value of the drift:\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\n\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.0, 1.0, length = 101), x in range(-1.8, 1.8, length = 101)]\n\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\nLagrangianDescriptorProblem for a SDEProblem\n  initial conditions: 10201 (101 × 101) of type Vector{Float64}\n  direction: :both\n\nThe Lagrangian descriptors are integrated along with the forward and backward solutions by \"solving\" the LagrangianDescriptorProblem, with any SDE algorithm of StochasticDiffEq.jl, here the adaptive SOSRI(), for diagonal noise:\n\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, SOSRI())\nLagrangianDescriptorSolution\n  initial conditions: 10201 (101 × 101) of type Vector{Float64}\n  direction: :both\n\nBy default, each initial condition is solved with its own realization of the noise, and the forward and backward directions are driven by independent noise, as for a two-sided Wiener process. We plot the forward and the backward Lagrangian descriptors, and their sum:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1200, 400))\nfor (i, direction) in enumerate((:forward, :backward, :total))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i]; title = string(direction), xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"y\")\n    heatmap!(ax, lagsol, direction)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe forward descriptors show the stable manifold of the saddle point, and the backward ones its unstable manifold, as for the deterministic equation, while the noise, independent from one initial condition to the next, makes the pictures slightly grainy.","title":"Duffing equation with additive noise"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial_SDEs/#A-single-realization-of-the-noise","page":"SDEs","text":"Following Balibrea-Iniesta et al. (2016), the Lagrangian descriptors of a single realization of the noise are computed with the same noise path for all initial conditions, with the keyword shared_noise = true. With the keyword realizations, the problem is solved for several realizations of the noise, and the solution gives the mean of the descriptors over them:\n\nlagsol_path = solve(lagprob, SOSRI(); shared_noise = true)\nlagsol_mean = solve(lagprob, SOSRI(); shared_noise = true, realizations = 10)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"a single realization\", xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, lagsol_path)\nax = Axis(fig[1, 2]; title = \"mean over 10 realizations\", xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, lagsol_mean)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nFor a single realization, the saddle point and the homoclinic loops are slightly moved and deformed by the noise, differently in each realization. Their mean shows the structures shared by the realizations.\n\nSee also the Stochastic Duffing equation example, with a multiplicative noise that keeps the saddle point in place, and the descriptor of Balibrea-Iniesta et al. (2016), given by the increments of the solutions, available as IncrementDescriptor.","title":"A single realization of the noise"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/lorenz/#Lorenz-system","page":"Lorenz system","text":"The Lorenz system\n\nbegincases\n  dot x = sigma (y - x) \n  dot y = x (rho - z) - y \n  dot z = x y - beta z\nendcases\n\nwith sigma = 10, rho = 28 and beta = 83, has a chaotic attractor. Its phase space is three-dimensional, so we compute the Lagrangian descriptors on a two-dimensional slice of initial conditions, here the plane z = 27, through the nontrivial fixed points (pmsqrt72 pmsqrt72 27). The plot recipe recognizes the grid in the components that vary, x and y.\n\nBackward in time, the volumes of phase space expand and the solutions diverge, so we only compute the forward descriptors, which reveal the stable manifolds.\n\nusing LagrangianDescriptors, OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nfunction lorenz!(du, u, p, t)\n    σ, ρ, β = p\n    x, y, z = u\n    du[1] = σ * (y - x)\n    du[2] = x * (ρ - z) - y\n    du[3] = x * y - β * z\nend\n\nprob = ODEProblem(lorenz!, zeros(3), (0.0, 1.5), (10.0, 28.0, 8 / 3))\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = [[x, y, 27.0] for y in range(-25, 25, length = 201), x in range(-20, 20, length = 201)]\n\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0; direction = :forward), Tsit5())\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (title = \"slice z = 27\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"forward Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe dark regions around the fixed points are the solutions that stay close to them, and the sharp curves are the intersections of the stable manifold of the origin, which separates the two wings of the attractor, with the plane. With a longer time window, more of its folds appear, but they also become thinner.\n\nOther slices are obtained with other collections of initial conditions, and the keyword idxs chooses the components to plot. For instance, on the plane y = 0:\n\nuu0_xz = [[x, 0.0, z] for z in range(0, 50, length = 201), x in range(-20, 20, length = 201)]\n\nlagsol_xz = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0_xz; direction = :forward), Tsit5())\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol_xz; idxs = (1, 3), axis = (title = \"slice y = 0\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"z\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"forward Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe two slices can also be seen together in three dimensions, with three components in idxs, and a common colorrange, so that their colors are comparable. CairoMakie, however, draws each plot over the previous ones, without hiding the parts that should be behind, so we split each plane at the line where they intersect, here through the middle of both, and draw the four half-planes from the farthest to the nearest, for the default view of Axis3. (GLMakie and WGLMakie hide the parts behind by themselves.)\n\nsolve_forward(uu0) = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0; direction = :forward), Tsit5())\n\nxs = range(-20, 20, length = 201)\n# the plane z = 27, split at y = 0, and the plane y = 0, split at z = 27\nback_of_z27 = solve_forward([[x, y, 27.0] for y in range(0, 27, length = 101), x in xs])\nfront_of_z27 = solve_forward([[x, y, 27.0] for y in range(-27, 0, length = 101), x in xs])\ntop_of_y0 = solve_forward([[x, 0.0, z] for z in range(27, 54, length = 101), x in xs])\nbottom_of_y0 = solve_forward([[x, 0.0, z] for z in range(0, 27, length = 101), x in xs])\n\n# from the farthest to the nearest\nhalves = (back_of_z27, bottom_of_y0, top_of_y0, front_of_z27)\ncolorrange = extrema(reduce(vcat, [lagsol() for lagsol in halves]))\n\nfig = Figure(size = (700, 550))\nax = Axis3(fig[1, 1]; title = \"slices z = 27 and y = 0\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\", zlabel = \"z\", viewmode = :fit)\nplts = [plot!(ax, lagsol; idxs = (1, 2, 3), colorrange) for lagsol in halves]\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plts[1]; label = \"forward Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nAny of the half-planes can be hidden, e.g. with plts[4].visible = false for the front one, to see the ones behind it. The axis then only spans the visible ones, unless its limits are given, e.g. with limits = ((-20, 20), (-27, 27), (0, 54)) in Axis3.","title":"Lorenz system"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/lorenz/#Initial-conditions-on-the-wings-of-the-attractor","page":"Lorenz system","text":"The initial conditions need not be on a plane. Each wing of the Lorenz attractor is close to a plane, and the two planes meet along a line below the attractor, forming an inverted wedge. We find these planes from a long solution on the attractor: the plane of each wing passes through the mean of its points, and its normal is the direction in which they vary the least, given by a singular value decomposition.\n\nusing LinearAlgebra: svd, normalize, cross, dot\n\n# a long solution on the attractor, without its transient\nsol = solve(remake(prob; u0 = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0], tspan = (0.0, 100.0)), Tsit5(); saveat = 0.01)\npoints = sol.u[sol.t .> 10]\npoints_pos = filter(u -> u[1] > 0, points) # the wing with x > 0\npoints_neg = filter(u -> u[1] < 0, points) # the wing with x < 0\n\n# the plane of a wing, through the mean `c` of its points, with normal `n`\nfunction wing_plane(points)\n    c = sum(points) / length(points)\n    n = svd(reduce(hcat, points .- Ref(c))').V[:, 3]\n    return (; c, n)\nend\nplane_pos = wing_plane(points_pos)\nplane_neg = wing_plane(points_neg)\n\n# the line where the two planes meet, through `o` in the direction `d`\nd = normalize(cross(plane_pos.n, plane_neg.n))\no = [plane_pos.n'; plane_neg.n'; d'] \\ [dot(plane_pos.n, plane_pos.c), dot(plane_neg.n, plane_neg.c), 0.0]\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n  0.025189554093966147\n  0.09515067623710814\n 15.050628363075255\n\nThe two planes meet along the line through o, at the height z approx 15, in the horizontal direction d. On each plane, we take a grid of initial conditions starting at this line and covering the points of the wing, and we compute the forward descriptors:\n\n# a grid on the plane of a wing, from the line where the planes meet, covering its points\nfunction wing_grid(plane, points)\n    w = normalize((plane.c - o) - dot(plane.c - o, d) * d) # in the plane, away from the line\n    along = extrema(dot(u - o, d) for u in points)\n    away = maximum(dot(u - o, w) for u in points)\n    return [o + a * d + b * w for b in range(0, away, length = 101), a in range(along..., length = 151)]\nend\n\nlagsol_pos = solve_forward(wing_grid(plane_pos, points_pos))\nlagsol_neg = solve_forward(wing_grid(plane_neg, points_neg))\ncolorrange = extrema(vcat(lagsol_pos(), lagsol_neg()))\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1200, 650))\naxes = [Axis3(fig[1, k]; azimuth, elevation = 0.1π, aspect = :data, viewmode = :fit,\n        xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\", zlabel = \"z\", xticks = -20:20:20, yticks = -20:20:20)\n    for (k, azimuth) in enumerate((1.45π, 1.7π))]\n# from these views, the wing with x < 0 is the nearest, so it is drawn last\nplts = [plot!(ax, lagsol; idxs = (1, 2, 3), colorrange) for ax in axes for lagsol in (lagsol_pos, lagsol_neg)]\nColorbar(fig[1, 3], plts[1]; label = \"forward Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nOn each wing, the dark spot is the fixed point of that wing, and the descriptors show the folds of the stable manifold of the origin crossing the wing, around which the solutions turn before switching to the other wing.","title":"Initial conditions on the wings of the attractor"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/lorenz/#Sweeping-through-the-attractor","page":"Lorenz system","text":"Two sequences of slices sweep through the attractor: horizontal slices z = c, for increasing values of c, and then vertical slices x = c, also for increasing values of c. We compute the forward descriptors on each slice, with a common color range, so that the slices are comparable:\n\nheights = 5:45     # the horizontal slices z = c\nabscissas = -20:20 # the vertical slices x = c\nxs = range(-20, 20, length = 151)\nys = range(-25, 25, length = 151)\nzs = range(0, 50, length = 151)\nhorizontal = [solve_forward([[x, y, c] for y in ys, x in xs]) for c in heights]\nvertical = [solve_forward([[c, y, z] for z in zs, y in ys]) for c in abscissas]\ncolorrange = extrema(reduce(vcat, [lagsol() for lagsol in [horizontal; vertical]]))\n\nWe show them in a video, with each slice next to the attractor, cut by the plane of the slice. The current frame is an Observable, which updates the figure in each frame recorded with record, with a plane and a heatmap for the horizontal slices, and others for the vertical ones, shown in turn. Since CairoMakie draws each plot over the previous ones, the part of the attractor behind the plane is drawn before it, and the part in front of it after it, with NaN to break the lines:\n\n# the frames: the horizontal slices, and then the vertical ones\nframes = [[(:z, i) for i in eachindex(heights)]; [(:x, i) for i in eachindex(abscissas)]]\nframe = Observable(frames[1])\n\n# seen from above and from x > c, behind a horizontal plane is below it, and behind\n# a vertical plane x = c is x < c\nis_behind((direction, i), u) = direction == :z ? u[3] <= heights[i] : u[1] <= abscissas[i]\npoints_on_attractor = Point3f.(points) # from the long solution of the previous section\nbehind = @lift([is_behind($frame, u) ? u : Point3f(NaN) for u in points_on_attractor])\nin_front = @lift([is_behind($frame, u) ? Point3f(NaN) : u for u in points_on_attractor])\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 500))\nax3 = Axis3(fig[1, 1]; title = \"the attractor and the slice\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\", zlabel = \"z\",\n    azimuth = 1.7π, elevation = 0.12π, viewmode = :fit, xticks = -20:20:20, yticks = -20:20:20)\nlines!(ax3, behind; color = :gray30, linewidth = 0.5)\nzplane = mesh!(ax3, @lift(Rect3f(Vec3f(-20, -25, heights[$frame[1] == :z ? $frame[2] : 1]), Vec3f(40, 50, 0.2)));\n    color = (:orange, 0.4), shading = false)\nxplane = mesh!(ax3, @lift(Rect3f(Vec3f(abscissas[$frame[1] == :x ? $frame[2] : 1], -25, 0), Vec3f(0.2, 50, 50)));\n    color = (:orange, 0.4), shading = false)\nlines!(ax3, in_front; color = :gray30, linewidth = 0.5)\n\nax = Axis(fig[1, 2]; aspect = DataAspect(),\n    title = @lift($frame[1] == :z ? \"slice z = $(heights[$frame[2]])\" : \"slice x = $(abscissas[$frame[2]])\"),\n    xlabel = @lift($frame[1] == :z ? \"x\" : \"y\"), ylabel = @lift($frame[1] == :z ? \"y\" : \"z\"))\nzheatmap = heatmap!(ax, @lift(horizontal[$frame[1] == :z ? $frame[2] : 1]); colorrange)\nxheatmap = heatmap!(ax, @lift(vertical[$frame[1] == :x ? $frame[2] : 1]); idxs = (2, 3), colorrange)\nColorbar(fig[1, 3], zheatmap; label = \"forward Lagrangian descriptor\")\ncolgap!(fig.layout, 1, 50) # room between the z label of the 3D axis and the heatmap\n\nrecord(fig, \"lorenz_sweeps.mp4\", frames; framerate = 8, px_per_unit = 1) do f\n    frame[] = f\n    zplane.visible = zheatmap.visible = f[1] == :z\n    xplane.visible = xheatmap.visible = f[1] == :x\n    reset_limits!(ax)\nend\n\n<video src=\"lorenz_sweeps.mp4\" autoplay loop muted playsinline controls style=\"width: 100%\"></video>\n\nAt the bottom of the attractor, the horizontal slices cut the stable manifold of the origin, a surface containing the z axis, along a single curve through the origin. Going up, they cross the two wings, and the folds of this manifold wind around the dark regions of each wing, with the whole pattern turning as z increases. The vertical slices then cross the wings from the side, one after the other, with the dark region of each wing and the folds around it.","title":"Sweeping through the attractor"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#plotting","page":"Plotting","text":"A LagrangianDescriptorSolution can be plotted with Makie.jl, through a package extension that is loaded together with Makie, or with Plots.jl, through a plot recipe. The rest of this documentation uses Makie, as the other packages of JuliaDynamics do, but both give the same kinds of plots, with the same arguments.","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#What-is-plotted","page":"Plotting","text":"plot(lagsol)                         # the default direction\nplot(lagsol, :forward)               # a given direction\nplot(lagsol; idxs = (1, 3))          # components 1 and 3 of the initial conditions\nplot(lagsol; idxs = (1, 2, 3))       # a three-dimensional plot of components 1, 2 and 3\n\nThe direction can be :forward, :backward, :both or :total (the sum of both, the default for a problem with both directions), or :difference (forward minus backward). When solved with several realizations, the mean over the realizations is plotted. The kind of plot depends on the collection uu0 of initial conditions. In two dimensions:\n\nnumbers (scalar problems), or vectors with a single component: a line with the Lagrangian descriptor at each initial condition;\na matrix of vectors forming a grid in the components idxs, in either layout, [[x, y] for y in ys, x in xs] or [[x, y] for x in xs, y in ys]: a heatmap;\nany other collection of vectors (scattered points, a curve, a matrix not forming a grid, ...): a scatter plot of the components idxs of the initial conditions, colored by the Lagrangian descriptor.\n\nThe keyword idxs, with default (1, 2), chooses the two components of the initial conditions plotted, e.g. for a two-dimensional slice of initial conditions of a higher-dimensional system. With three components, the plot is three-dimensional; see Three-dimensional plots.\n\nThe colormap is :inferno by default, with both Makie and Plots.\n\nAs an example, we use the Lagrangian descriptors of the Duffing equation ddot x = x - x^3, on a grid of initial conditions.","title":"What is plotted"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#With-Makie","page":"Plotting","text":"using OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\n\nfunction f!(du, u, p, t)\n    x, y = u\n    du[1] = y\n    du[2] = x - x^3\nend\n\nprob = ODEProblem(f!, [0.0, 0.0], (0.0, 5.0))\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.0, 1.0, length = 201), x in range(-1.6, 1.6, length = 321)]\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5())\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nAny compatible plot function can be used, with the usual Makie attributes, e.g. a given direction, with a different colormap, and filled contours:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"forward\", xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\")\nplt = heatmap!(ax, lagsol, :forward; colormap = :viridis)\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt)\nax = Axis(fig[1, 3]; title = \"backward\", xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\")\nplt = contourf!(ax, lagsol, :backward; levels = 20, colormap = :plasma)\nColorbar(fig[1, 4], plt)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe default colormap of the package takes precedence over a colormap set with Makie's set_theme!, so another colormap is given with colormap = ... in each call.","title":"With Makie"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#With-Plots","page":"Plotting","text":"using OrdinaryDiffEq, Plots\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\n\nfunction f!(du, u, p, t)\n    x, y = u\n    du[1] = y\n    du[2] = x - x^3\nend\n\nprob = ODEProblem(f!, [0.0, 0.0], (0.0, 5.0))\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.0, 1.0, length = 201), x in range(-1.6, 1.6, length = 321)]\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5())\n\nplot(lagsol; xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"ẋ\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nAny series type and the usual Plots attributes can be used, e.g. a given direction, with a different colormap, and filled contours:\n\nplot(\n    plot(lagsol, :forward; title = \"forward\", color = :viridis),\n    plot(lagsol, :backward; title = \"backward\", seriestype = :contourf, levels = 20, color = :plasma);\n    layout = (1, 2), size = (1000, 400), margin = 5Plots.mm, xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"ẋ\",\n)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nWith Plots, the colormap can also be changed for all plots with Plots.default(color = :viridis).","title":"With Plots"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Three-dimensional-plots","page":"Plotting","text":"With three components in idxs, e.g. idxs = (1, 2, 3), the plot is three-dimensional, in these components of the initial conditions:\n\na matrix of initial conditions is seen as a parametrized surface, not necessarily a plane, and drawn as a surface colored by the Lagrangian descriptor;\nany other collection of initial conditions is drawn as a three-dimensional scatter plot, colored by the Lagrangian descriptor.\n\nWith Plots, both are drawn as three-dimensional scatter plots, since GR, the default backend of Plots, does not color a parametrized surface by other values.\n\nAs an example, we use the Arnold–Beltrami–Childress (ABC) flow, a steady three-dimensional flow with chaotic regions, on a sphere of initial conditions:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\n\nfunction abc!(du, u, p, t)\n    A, B, C = p\n    x, y, z = u\n    du[1] = A * sin(z) + C * cos(y)\n    du[2] = B * sin(x) + A * cos(z)\n    du[3] = C * sin(y) + B * cos(x)\nend\n\nprob = ODEProblem(abc!, zeros(3), (0.0, 10.0), (sqrt(3), sqrt(2), 1.0))\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\n\n# a sphere of radius 1.5 around (π, π, π), parametrized by the angles θ and φ\nsphere(θ, φ) = [π + 1.5sin(θ) * cos(φ), π + 1.5sin(θ) * sin(φ), π + 1.5cos(θ)]\nuu0 = [sphere(θ, φ) for θ in range(0, π, length = 101), φ in range(0, 2π, length = 201)]\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5())\n\nfig, ax, plt = plot(lagsol; idxs = (1, 2, 3), figure = (size = (600, 500),),\n    axis = (xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\", zlabel = \"z\", aspect = :data, viewmode = :fit))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nWith Makie, the surface is drawn without lighting, so that its colors are those of the colorbar; shading = true turns the lighting on. Several solutions can be drawn on the same axis, e.g. with plot!(ax, lagsol; idxs = (1, 2, 3)) on an Axis3. The colors of each are scaled separately, unless a common colorrange is given. CairoMakie draws each plot over the previous ones, so intersecting surfaces are split at their intersection and drawn from the farthest to the nearest, as in the Lorenz system example; GLMakie and WGLMakie hide the parts behind by themselves.\n\nWith Plots, the same sphere, with fewer initial conditions, gives a scatter plot:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, Plots\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\n\nfunction abc!(du, u, p, t)\n    A, B, C = p\n    x, y, z = u\n    du[1] = A * sin(z) + C * cos(y)\n    du[2] = B * sin(x) + A * cos(z)\n    du[3] = C * sin(y) + B * cos(x)\nend\n\nprob = ODEProblem(abc!, zeros(3), (0.0, 10.0), (sqrt(3), sqrt(2), 1.0))\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\n\nsphere(θ, φ) = [π + 1.5sin(θ) * cos(φ), π + 1.5sin(θ) * sin(φ), π + 1.5cos(θ)]\nuu0 = [sphere(θ, φ) for θ in range(0, π, length = 51), φ in range(0, 2π, length = 101)]\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5())\n\nplot(lagsol; idxs = (1, 2, 3), title = \"Lagrangian descriptor\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\", zlabel = \"z\", markersize = 3)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"Three-dimensional plots"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#With-both","page":"Plotting","text":"If both Plots and Makie are loaded, their plotting functions must be qualified, e.g. Makie.heatmap(lagsol) or Plots.plot(lagsol).","title":"With both"},{"category":"section","location":"#LagrangianDescriptors.jl","page":"Overview","text":"","title":"LagrangianDescriptors.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-started","page":"Overview","text":"The method of Lagrangian descriptors explains the method and how the package computes the descriptors.\nChoosing the descriptor and the time window shows the effect of the choices that shape the pictures, with some guidance on them.\nThe tutorials on ordinary, random and stochastic differential equations go through the use of the package step by step, and Alternative via post-processing compares it with computing the descriptors from solutions of the original equations.\nThe Examples show the method on classical systems, from simple ones to chaotic and stochastic ones.\nThe API documents all functions and types.\nThe References list the literature on the method.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-example","page":"Overview","text":"Here is the code that computes the Lagrangian descriptors in the image above, of a periodically forced Duffing equation, revealing some of the structures of its dynamics.\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\n\nfunction f!(du, u, p, t)\n    x, y = u\n    A, ω = p\n    du[1] = y\n    du[2] = x - x^3 + A * sin(ω * t)\nend\n\nu0 = [0.5, 2.2]\ntspan = (0.0, 13.0)\nA = 5.0; ω = 2π; p = (A, ω);\nprob = ODEProblem(f!, u0, tspan, p)\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.5, -0.5, length=301), x in range(-0.4, 1.6, length=601)]\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\n\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5())\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"A first example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Developers","page":"Overview","text":"JuliaDynamics/LagrangianDescriptors.jl is currently being developed by Ricardo M. S. Rosa.","title":"Developers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Cite","page":"Overview","text":"Just cite the github repo JuliaDynamics/LagrangianDescriptors.jl for now, while the package is not yet registered.","title":"Cite"},{"category":"module","location":"#LagrangianDescriptors","page":"Overview","text":"LagrangianDescriptors.jl\n\n(Image: docsdev) (Image: docsstable) (Image: CI) (Image: codecov) (Image: License: MIT)\n\n(Image: Lagrangian descriptors of a periodically forced Duffing equation)\n\nLagrangianDescriptors.jl is a Julia package for painting the phase portrait of dynamical systems with the method of Lagrangian descriptors, which reveals singular features of the dynamics, such as stable and unstable manifolds, by coloring each initial condition according to the solution through it. It provides\n\nLagrangian descriptors of ordinary, random and stochastic differential equations, given as ODEProblems, RODEProblems and SDEProblems of the SciML ecosystem, forward and backward in time.\nDescriptors given by the integral of any infinitesimal descriptor along the solutions, or by the increments of the solutions, as for stochastic equations.\nMeans over realizations of the noise, with independent noise for each initial condition, or with the same noise path for all of them.\nStopping solutions that diverge, separately in each direction of time.\nPlots with Plots.jl or Makie.jl, as heatmaps of grids of initial conditions, scatter plots of other collections, two-dimensional slices of higher-dimensional systems, or in three dimensions, e.g. as surfaces of initial conditions.\n\n(Image: Lagrangian descriptors of the Lorenz system on slices sweeping through its attractor)\n\nThe package is not registered yet. To install it, run import Pkg; Pkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/JuliaDynamics/LagrangianDescriptors.jl\").\n\nAll further information is provided in the documentation, which you can find online or build locally by running the docs/make.jl file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianDescriptors"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/stochastic_duffing/#Stochastic-Duffing-equation","page":"Stochastic Duffing equation","text":"The Duffing equation with a multiplicative noise in the velocity, proportional to the cube root of the position,\n\nbegincases\n  mathrmdx = y mathrmdt \n  mathrmdy = (x - x^3) mathrmdt + sigma x^13 mathrmdW_t\nendcases\n\nwhere W_t is a Wiener process, is a stochastic perturbation of the Duffing equation of the page on choosing the descriptor and the time window. Unlike the additive noise of the tutorial on SDEs, this noise vanishes on the line x = 0, so the origin stays an equilibrium, a saddle point, for every realization of the noise, while the other structures are perturbed. We set it up as an SDEProblem, with diagonal noise, which is zero in the first component, with the real cube root cbrt, also for negative values:\n\nusing LagrangianDescriptors, StochasticDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\ndrift(u, p, t) = [u[2], u[1] - u[1]^3]\ndiffusion(u, σ, t) = [0.0, σ * cbrt(u[1])]\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du) # with `du` the drift\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1, 1, length = 101), x in range(-1.8, 1.8, length = 101)]\n\nFollowing Balibrea-Iniesta et al. (2016), the Lagrangian descriptors of a single realization of the noise are computed with the same noise path for all initial conditions, with the keyword shared_noise = true of solve. By default, each initial condition has independent noise. The seed of the SDEProblem makes the computation reproducible:\n\nprob = SDEProblem(drift, diffusion, [0.0, 0.0], (0.0, 5.0), 0.2; seed = 123)\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\nlagsol_path = solve(lagprob, EM(), dt = 0.01; shared_noise = true)\n\nThe pictures of different realizations differ, and the mean over realizations shows the structures they share. With the keyword realizations, the problem is solved for several realizations, each with its own noise path, shared by all initial conditions, and the solution gives the mean of the descriptors:\n\nlagsol_mean = solve(lagprob, EM(), dt = 0.01; shared_noise = true, realizations = 10)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"a single realization\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, lagsol_path)\nax = Axis(fig[1, 2]; title = \"mean over 10 realizations\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, lagsol_mean)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nFor a single realization, the saddle point stays at the origin, where the noise vanishes, while the homoclinic loops are deformed by the noise, and they change from one realization to another. Their mean shows the structures shared by the realizations.\n\nWithout shared_noise = true, each initial condition has independent noise, also in each realization. The mean over realizations approximates the same mean at each initial condition, but, with few realizations, the picture is grainier, since the noise changes from one initial condition to the next.\n\nThe descriptor of Balibrea-Iniesta et al. (2016), given by the increments of the solutions, is available as IncrementDescriptor.","title":"Stochastic Duffing equation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/vanderpol/#Van-der-Pol-oscillator","page":"Van der Pol oscillator","text":"The van der Pol oscillator\n\nbegincases\n  dot x = y \n  dot y = mu (1 - x^2) y - x\nendcases\n\nwith mu  0, has an unstable focus at the origin and an attracting limit cycle. Forward in time, all solutions approach the limit cycle. Backward in time, the solutions inside the limit cycle approach the origin, while those outside grow without bound.\n\nThe solutions that diverge backward in time need to be stopped, with the keyword max_trajectory_value of solve. The forward and backward solutions are stopped separately, so the forward descriptors are not affected:\n\nusing LagrangianDescriptors, OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nfunction vanderpol!(du, u, μ, t)\n    x, y = u\n    du[1] = y\n    du[2] = μ * (1 - x^2) * y - x\nend\n\nprob = ODEProblem(vanderpol!, [0.0, 0.0], (0.0, 10.0), 1.0)\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nxs = range(-3, 3, length = 201)\nys = range(-4, 4, length = 201)\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in ys, x in xs]\n\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5(); max_trajectory_value = 10.0)\n\ncount(lagsol.terminated_forward), count(lagsol.terminated_backward)\n(0, 28974)\n\nThe descriptors of the stopped backward solutions only measure how fast they diverge, so we leave them out of the backward picture, setting them to NaN:\n\nLbwd = lagsol(:backward)\nLbwd[lagsol.terminated_backward] .= NaN\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"forward\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\nheatmap!(ax, lagsol, :forward)\nax = Axis(fig[1, 2]; title = \"backward, bounded solutions\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\n# Makie's `heatmap(xs, ys, z)` has `z[i, j]` at `(xs[i], ys[j])`, hence the `permutedims`\nheatmap!(ax, xs, ys, permutedims(reshape(Lbwd, size(uu0))))\nfig\nFigure()\n\nForward in time, all solutions approach the limit cycle, and the descriptor only shows the unstable focus at the origin. Backward in time, the limit cycle is repelling, and it appears as the boundary of the region of solutions that remain bounded.","title":"Van der Pol oscillator"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial_RODEs/#Random-Ordinary-Differential-Equations","page":"RODEs","text":"We consider, now, some examples of applying the Lagrangian descriptor method to equations of the type RODEProblem.","title":"Random Ordinary Differential Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial_RODEs/#Random-Duffing-equation","page":"RODEs","text":"We consider, first, the random Duffing equation\n\nddot x = x - x^3 + asin(W_t)\n\nwhere W_t_t is a standard Wiener process.\n\nWith LagrangianDescriptors.jl, we i) start by setting up the equation as a RODEProblem; next, we ii) wrap that as a LagrangianDescriptorProblem; then we iii) solve it; and finally, we iv) plot the result with the built-in plot recipe. Each initial condition is solved with its own realization of the noise, and the forward and backward directions are driven by independent noise, as for a two-sided Wiener process.\n\nSo we first load the relevant packages:\n\nusing StochasticDiffEq\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\n\nNext we set up the RODEProblem, with the default noise, a standard Wiener process, here with a single component, given by rand_prototype, and a seed for reproducible results:\n\nfunction f!(du, u, p, t, W)\n    x, y = u\n    du[1] = y\n    du[2] = x - x^3 + p * sin(W[1])\nend\n\np = 0.025\nu0 = [0.5, 2.2]\ntspan = (0.0, 8.0)\n\nprob = RODEProblem(f!, u0, tspan, p; rand_prototype = [0.0], seed = 123)\n\u001b[38;2;86;182;194mRODEProblem\u001b[0m with uType \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mVector{Float64}\u001b[0m and tType \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mFloat64\u001b[0m. In-place: \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mtrue\u001b[0m\nNon-trivial mass matrix: \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mfalse\u001b[0m\ntimespan: (0.0, 8.0)\nu0: 2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.5\n 2.2\n\nWith the RODE problem setup, we choose an infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor, a collection of initial conditions on the phase space, which is the region to be \"painted\", and finally we build the LagrangianDescriptorProblem:\n\nM(du, u, p, t, W) = norm(du)\n\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.0, 1.0, length=301), x in range(-1.8, 1.8, length=301)]\n\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\nLagrangianDescriptorProblem for a RODEProblem\n  initial conditions: 90601 (301 × 301) of type Vector{Float64}\n  direction: :both\n\nThe Lagrangian descriptors are the time-integration of the infinitesimal descriptor along forward and backward solutions of the equation. They are integrated along with the solutions by \"solving\" the LagrangianDescriptorProblem, with an overload of the solve method from the SciML ecosystem:\n\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, RandomHeun(), dt=1/100)\nLagrangianDescriptorSolution\n  initial conditions: 90601 (301 × 301) of type Vector{Float64}\n  direction: :both\n\nWith the solution at hand, we plot the Lagrangian descriptors to visualize the dynamics of the system:\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (title = \"Random Duffing equation, a = $p\", xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Random Duffing equation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/saddle/#Saddle-point","page":"Saddle point","text":"The linear system\n\nbegincases\n  dot x = x \n  dot y = -y\nendcases\n\nhas a saddle point at the origin, with the y-axis as its stable manifold and the x-axis as its unstable manifold. Since the solutions are known explicitly, x(t) = x_0 e^t and y(t) = y_0 e^-t, this is a good first check of the method.\n\nusing LagrangianDescriptors, OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nsaddle(u, p, t) = [u[1], -u[2]]\nprob = ODEProblem(saddle, [0.0, 0.0], (0.0, 2.0))\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1, 1, length = 201), x in range(-1, 1, length = 201)]\n\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5())\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1200, 400))\nfor (i, direction) in enumerate((:forward, :backward, :total))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i]; title = string(direction), xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\")\n    heatmap!(ax, lagsol, direction)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe forward descriptor is smallest along the stable manifold x = 0, where the solutions approach the saddle, and grows with x_0, since the solutions leave the saddle exponentially fast. The backward descriptor, likewise, reveals the unstable manifold y = 0, and their sum shows both.","title":"Saddle point"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"The method of Lagrangian descriptors was introduced by Jiménez Madrid and Mancho (2009) and developed by Mancho et al. (2013), with a theoretical framework given by Lopesino et al. (2017) and an extension to stochastic differential equations by Balibrea-Iniesta et al. (2016). For an introduction to the method, see Wiggins and García-Garrido (2022), and for a comprehensive account, with many examples, see the online book by Agaoglou et al. (2020). Answers to frequently asked questions about the method are collected by Mancho (2016).\n\nAgaoglou, M.; Aguilar-Sanjuan, B.; García Garrido, V. J.; González-Montoya, F.; Katsanikas, M.; Krajňák, V.; Naik, S. and Wiggins, S. (2020). Lagrangian Descriptors: Discovery and Quantification of Phase Space Structure and Transport (The CHAMPS Project).\n\n\n\nBalibrea-Iniesta, F.; Lopesino, C.; Wiggins, S. and Mancho, A. M. (2016). Lagrangian Descriptors for Stochastic Differential Equations: A Tool for Revealing the Phase Portrait of Stochastic Dynamical Systems. International Journal of Bifurcation and Chaos 26, 1630036.\n\n\n\nGarcía-Garrido, V. J. and García-Luengo, J. (2022). Painting the phase space of dissipative systems with Lagrangian descriptors. Communications in Nonlinear Science and Numerical Simulation 104, 106034.\n\n\n\nJiménez Madrid, J. A. and Mancho, A. M. (2009). Distinguished trajectories in time dependent vector fields. Chaos: An Interdisciplinary Journal of Nonlinear Science 19, 013111.\n\n\n\nLopesino, C.; Balibrea-Iniesta, F.; García-Garrido, V. J.; Wiggins, S. and Mancho, A. M. (2017). A Theoretical Framework for Lagrangian Descriptors. International Journal of Bifurcation and Chaos 27, 1730001.\n\n\n\nMancho, A. M. (2016). Frequently Asked Questions about Lagrangian Descriptors. Interactive comment SC2 in the discussion of acp-2016-633, Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics.\n\n\n\nMancho, A. M.; Wiggins, S.; Curbelo, J. and Mendoza, C. (2013). Lagrangian descriptors: A method for revealing phase space structures of general time dependent dynamical systems. Communications in Nonlinear Science and Numerical Simulation 18, 3530–3557.\n\n\n\nShadden, S. C.; Lekien, F. and Marsden, J. E. (2005). Definition and properties of Lagrangian coherent structures from finite-time Lyapunov exponents in two-dimensional aperiodic flows. Physica D: Nonlinear Phenomena 212, 271–304.\n\n\n\nWiggins, S. and García-Garrido, V. (2022). Painting the Phase Portrait of a Dynamical System with the Computational Tool of Lagrangian Descriptors. Notices of the American Mathematical Society 69.\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial_ODEs/#Ordinary-Differential-Equations","page":"ODEs","text":"We consider, here, some examples of applying the Lagrangian descriptor method to equations of the type ODEProblem.","title":"Ordinary Differential Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial_ODEs/#Periodically-forced-Duffing-equation","page":"ODEs","text":"We start with an application of the method of Lagrangian descriptors to the periodically-forced Duffing equation, as illustrated by Wiggins and García-Garrido (2022). The equation takes the form\n\nddot x = x - x^3 + Asin(omega t)\n\nWe write it as a system\n\nbegincases\n  dot x = y \n  dot y = x - x^3 + Asin(omega t)\nendcases\n\nThe idea is to set up this system as an ODEProblem from SciML/DifferentialEquations.jl, then wrap it as a LagrangianDescriptorProblem from LagrangianDescriptors.jl, which we can then solve as an ensemble problem and plot the result.\n\nSo we first load the relevant packages:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\n\nNext we set up the ODEProblem:\n\nfunction f!(du, u, p, t)\n    x, y = u\n    A, ω = p\n    du[1] = y\n    du[2] = x - x^3 + A * sin(ω * t)\nend\n\nu0 = [0.5, 2.2]\ntspan = (0.0, 13.0)\nA = 0.3; ω = π; p = (A, ω)\n\nprob = ODEProblem(f!, u0, tspan, p)\n\u001b[38;2;86;182;194mODEProblem\u001b[0m with uType \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mVector{Float64}\u001b[0m and tType \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mFloat64\u001b[0m. In-place: \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mtrue\u001b[0m\nNon-trivial mass matrix: \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mfalse\u001b[0m\ntimespan: (0.0, 13.0)\nu0: 2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.5\n 2.2\n\nWith the ODE problem setup, we choose an infinitesimal Lagrangian descriptor, a collection of initial conditions on the phase space, which is the region to be \"painted\", and finally we build the LagrangianDescriptorProblem:\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\n\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.0, 1.0, length=301), x in range(-1.8, 1.8, length=301)]\n\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\nLagrangianDescriptorProblem for an ODEProblem\n  initial conditions: 90601 (301 × 301) of type Vector{Float64}\n  direction: :both\n\nThe Lagrangian descriptors are the time-integration of the infinitesimal descriptor along forward and backward solutions of the equation. They are integrated along with the solutions by \"solving\" the LagrangianDescriptorProblem, with an overload of the solve method from the SciML ecosystem:\n\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5())\nLagrangianDescriptorSolution\n  initial conditions: 90601 (301 × 301) of type Vector{Float64}\n  direction: :both\n\nWith the solution at hand, we plot the Lagrangian descriptors to visualize the dynamics of the system:\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (title = \"Duffing equation, A = $A, ω = $(round(ω, digits = 3))\",\n    xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nWe may zoom closer to the origin to find the following \"painting\":\n\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-0.6, 0.3, length=601), x in range(-0.2, 0.4, length=401)]\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\n\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5());\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (title = \"Duffing equation, A = $A, ω = $(round(ω, digits = 3))\",\n    xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nIf we want to change parameters, we just remake the original ODEProblem:\n\nA = 2.0; ω = 2π; p = (A, ω);\nprob = remake(prob, p=p)\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-0.6, -0.1, length=501), x in range(-0.2, 0.3, length=401)]\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\n\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5());\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (title = \"Duffing equation, A = $A, ω = $(round(ω, digits = 3))\",\n    xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Periodically-forced Duffing equation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial_ODEs/#Autonomous-Duffing-equation","page":"ODEs","text":"For visualizing the autonomous case, we may rewrite the system or, since we already have the non-autonomous case implemented, we just set the amplitude of the forcing term to zero:\n\nA = 0.0; ω = 2π; p = (A, ω);\nprob = remake(prob, p=p)\nuu0 = [[x, y] for y in range(-1.0, 1.0, length=301), x in range(-1.8, 1.8, length=301)]\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\n\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5());\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (title = \"Duffing equation, A = $A, ω = $(round(ω, digits = 3))\",\n    xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Autonomous Duffing equation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial_ODEs/#A-scalar-cubic-equation","page":"ODEs","text":"Although one-dimensional problems are easier to understand, the method also works for such problems. Here we experiment with the scalar cubic equation\n\n   fracmathrmdxmathrmdt = x - x^3\n\nThis equation has three stationary solutions, associated with the fixed points x=-1, x=0 and x=1.\n\nWe set up the ODEProblem, loading the packages first:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing LagrangianDescriptors\n\nf(u, p, t) = u - u^3\n\nu0 = 0.5\ntspan = (0.0, 1.0)\nprob = ODEProblem(f, u0, tspan)\n\u001b[38;2;86;182;194mODEProblem\u001b[0m with uType \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mFloat64\u001b[0m and tType \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mFloat64\u001b[0m. In-place: \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mfalse\u001b[0m\nNon-trivial mass matrix: \u001b[38;2;86;182;194mfalse\u001b[0m\ntimespan: (0.0, 1.0)\nu0: 0.5\n\nBuild the LagrangianDescriptorProblem:\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = range(-1.0, 1.0, length = 201)\nlagprob = LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0)\nLagrangianDescriptorProblem for an ODEProblem\n  initial conditions: 201 of type Float64\n  direction: :both\n\nSolve it:\n\nlagsol = solve(lagprob, Tsit5())\nLagrangianDescriptorSolution\n  initial conditions: 201 of type Float64\n  direction: :both\n\nand plot it:\n\nfig = Figure()\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"Lagrangian descriptors for a cubic equation\", xlabel = L\"x_0\")\nfor direction in (:forward, :backward, :total, :difference)\n    lines!(ax, lagsol, direction; label = string(direction))\nend\naxislegend(ax; position = :ct)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nIn any of the Lagrangian descriptors (forward, backward, total, and difference) we distinguish the two fixed points.","title":"A scalar cubic equation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/pendulum/#Pendulum","page":"Pendulum","text":"The pendulum\n\nbegincases\n  dot theta = omega \n  dot omega = -sin theta\nendcases\n\nhas centers at theta = 2kpi, surrounded by oscillations, and saddles at theta = (2k + 1)pi, connected by heteroclinic orbits, the separatrices, which separate the oscillations from the rotations of the pendulum over the top.\n\nusing LagrangianDescriptors, OrdinaryDiffEq, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nfunction pendulum!(du, u, p, t)\n    θ, ω = u\n    du[1] = ω\n    du[2] = -sin(θ)\nend\n\nprob = ODEProblem(pendulum!, [0.0, 0.0], (0.0, 10.0))\n\nM(du, u, p, t) = norm(du)\nuu0 = [[θ, ω] for ω in range(-3, 3, length = 201), θ in range(-2π, 2π, length = 401)]\n\nlagsol = solve(LagrangianDescriptorProblem(prob, M, uu0), Tsit5())\n\nfig, ax, plt = heatmap(lagsol; axis = (xlabel = L\"\\theta\", ylabel = L\"\\omega\"))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], plt; label = \"Lagrangian descriptor\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe separatrices appear as sharp curves through the saddles at theta = pmpi. Inside them, the oscillations have small descriptors near the centers, where the solutions move slowly. Outside them, the rotations, which move faster, have larger descriptors.","title":"Pendulum"}]
}
